---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const sections = [
  { href: "#concepts", label: "Concepts" },
  { href: "#goals", label: "Goals & weekly targets" },
  { href: "#data", label: "Your data & backups" },
  { href: "#shortcuts", label: "Keyboard shortcuts" },
  { href: "#troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting" },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Docs"
  description="How Timeblock works: Plan vs. Actual, goals and weekly targets, your data and backups, keyboard shortcuts."
>
  <h1>Docs</h1>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="Docs sections">
      {sections.map((s) => <a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a>)}
    </nav>

    <div class="docs-content">
  <h2 id="concepts">Concepts: Plan, Actual, and ghosts</h2>
  <p>
    Every day has two lanes, side by side:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan</strong> — what you intend to do. Drag to create, move and resize blocks.
      <span class="kbd">Shift</span> while dragging snaps to 5-minute steps; <span class="kbd">Ctrl</span>
      copies a block instead of moving it. Overlapping blocks lay out side by side rather than
      hiding each other.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Actual</strong> — what really happened. You don't re-type this: every plan block
      appears as a dashed <strong>ghost</strong> in the Actual lane. Press <span class="kbd">a</span>
      (or click ✓) to confirm it happened as planned, or <span class="kbd">s</span> (✗) to skip it.
      Drag a ghost to log it at a different time than planned.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Unconfirmed time never counts as actual.</strong> That's the core honesty mechanic:
    a day isn't "100% on plan" just because you planned it that way — only confirmed or
    independently-logged time counts. When you're catching up at the end of the day,
    <span class="kbd">Shift+A</span> ("confirm rest of day") confirms every remaining past ghost
    around whatever you've already logged, instead of making you click through each one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Week view</strong> shows the same Plan | Actual split across all seven days, with
    past days editable and blocks draggable across day boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2 id="goals">Goals &amp; weekly targets</h2>
  <p>
    A goal (e.g. "Thesis", "Social") carries a weekly target that's either:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>At least</strong> — e.g. Thesis ≥ 15 hours a week.</li>
    <li><strong>At most</strong> — e.g. Social ≤ 3 hours a week.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Targets carry forward week to week until you change them, and past weeks never change
    retroactively — so your history reflects what the target actually was at the time, not
    what it is now. Each goal also has an outcome checklist per week (concrete things you
    wanted to get done), with carry-over for anything left unchecked.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>week budget panel</strong> (<span class="kbd">Ctrl+B</span>) shows target vs. plan vs.
    actual per goal as you work through the week. The <strong>weekly review</strong> is the
    end-of-week version of the same comparison: status per goal, outcomes, and a free-text
    reflection note.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    Templates and routines (saving a day/week as a reusable template, recurring plan blocks)
    are planned but not yet shipped — they'll be documented here once they land.
  </div>

  <h2 id="data">Your data &amp; backups</h2>
  <p>
    Timeblock's desktop app stores everything in a single SQLite file on your machine —
    no account, no server, no telemetry.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>OS</th><th>Folder</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>~/.local/share/com.shreysingh.timeblock/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>~/Library/Application Support/com.shreysingh.timeblock/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\com.shreysingh.timeblock\</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><code>data.sqlite3</code> holds everything. <em>Settings → Open data folder</em> opens it.</li>
    <li>
      <code>backups/</code> gets a copy at most once a day, before every import, and before
      schema upgrades; the newest 14 are kept. To restore one, quit Timeblock and copy it over
      <code>data.sqlite3</code> — or use <em>Settings → Restore a backup</em>, which backs up the
      current data first.
    </li>
    <li><em>Settings → Export JSON</em> writes a readable file you can keep or import elsewhere.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="shortcuts">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">d</span> <span class="kbd">w</span> <span class="kbd">g</span> <span class="kbd">r</span></td><td>Day, Week, Goals, Review</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">t</span>, <span class="kbd">[</span>, <span class="kbd">]</span></td><td>Today, previous, next</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Ctrl+Z</span> / <span class="kbd">Ctrl+Shift+Z</span></td><td>Undo / redo</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Ctrl+B</span></td><td>Week budget panel</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Enter</span></td><td>Edit the selected block</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Delete</span></td><td>Delete the selected block (skips a ghost)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">↑</span> <span class="kbd">↓</span> / <span class="kbd">Shift+↑</span> <span class="kbd">Shift+↓</span></td><td>Nudge / resize the selected block</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">1</span>–<span class="kbd">9</span></td><td>Set the selected block's goal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">a</span> / <span class="kbd">s</span></td><td>Confirm / skip the selected plan block or ghost</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Shift+A</span></td><td>Confirm the rest of the day as planned</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="kbd">Esc</span></td><td>Cancel a drag, close the editor, clear the selection</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>Blank or flickering window on Linux (Wayland, NVIDIA)</h3>
  <p>
    WebKitGTK and the proprietary NVIDIA driver don't always get along. Timeblock already
    sets <code>WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1</code> when the NVIDIA kernel module is
    loaded (unless you set it yourself). If the window is still blank or flickers, try these
    one at a time:
  </p>
  <pre><code>WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1 timeblock
__NV_DISABLE_EXPLICIT_SYNC=1 timeblock
GDK_BACKEND=x11 timeblock</code></pre>

  <p>
    More: <a href="https://github.com/9shrey/timeblock">source on GitHub</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/9shrey/timeblock/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">changelog</a>
  </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  :global(main:has(.docs-layout)) {
    max-width: 1100px;
  }
  .docs-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .docs-toc {
    position: sticky;
    top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .docs-toc a {
    display: block;
    padding: 0.3rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .docs-toc a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .docs-content {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .docs-layout {
      display: block;
    }
    .docs-toc {
      position: static;
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0 1rem;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
